{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-post-js","path":"/posts/hybrid-inference-with-firebase-ai-logic-on-device-first-cloud-fallback/","result":{"data":{"markdownRemark":{"html":"<p><span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 1280px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/00711/hero.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 33.4375%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <picture>\n          <source\n              srcset=\"/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/fb34c/hero.avif 320w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/646cd/hero.avif 768w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/8b485/hero.avif 1024w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/a73d6/hero.avif 1280w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/a7f9f/hero.avif 1920w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/fb732/hero.avif 2400w\"\n              sizes=\"(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px\"\n              type=\"image/avif\"\n            /><source\n              srcset=\"/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/cb523/hero.webp 320w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/cd067/hero.webp 768w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/53334/hero.webp 1024w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/4b075/hero.webp 1280w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/f3ff0/hero.webp 1920w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/4293a/hero.webp 2400w\"\n              sizes=\"(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px\"\n              type=\"image/webp\"\n            />\n          <source\n            srcset=\"/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/d4f27/hero.png 320w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/778aa/hero.png 768w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/658fc/hero.png 1024w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/26c69/hero.png 1280w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/7d442/hero.png 1920w,\n/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/00711/hero.png 2400w\"\n            sizes=\"(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px\"\n            type=\"image/png\"\n          />\n          <img\n            class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n            src=\"/static/e764fd53571ae8ea7dac3ce135402c53/26c69/hero.png\"\n            alt=\"hero\"\n            title=\"\"\n            loading=\"lazy\"\n            decoding=\"async\"\n            style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n          />\n        </picture>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>TL;DR; With the Firebase AI Logic web SDK, <code class=\"language-text\">getGenerativeModel(ai, { mode: InferenceMode.PREFER_ON_DEVICE })</code> runs Gemini Nano in Chrome when the device can, and calls a cloud Gemini model when it can't. Same code, same schema, and <code class=\"language-text\">response.inferenceSource</code> tells you which one answered. 🎉</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>This is the fourth post of my series <em>AI on the web in 2026</em>. In the <a href=\"/posts/structured-output-from-the-chrome-prompt-api-with-json-schema/\">previous post</a> I ended with \"if the device can't run the model, use the cloud\". Writing that branching by hand gets old fast: two SDKs, two request formats, two error paths.</p>\n<p>Firebase AI Logic has a hybrid mode for the web that does the branching for you. In this article, we'll explore the setup, the four inference modes, structured output on both paths, the limits of the on-device side, and why App Check is not optional.</p>\n<p>Keep in mind that hybrid experiences are a <strong>Preview</strong> feature in Firebase AI Logic. That means no SLA or deprecation policy, and the API can change in backwards-incompatible ways. Plan for it.</p>\n<h2>The Problem with two code paths</h2>\n<p>A feature that uses built-in AI in Chrome with a cloud fallback usually ends up like this:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One branch for <code class=\"language-text\">LanguageModel</code> with its own availability checks and download handling.</li>\n<li>Another branch for a cloud SDK with a different request and response shape.</li>\n<li>Prompts and schemas copied between both, slowly drifting apart.</li>\n<li>No easy way to know, in production, which path users actually hit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It works. It's also two features pretending to be one.</p>\n<h2>The Solution: one model, four modes</h2>\n<p>You need the Firebase JS SDK v12.5.0 or later, and Chrome on desktop, version 139 or higher, for the on-device side.</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"js\"><pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\"><span class=\"token keyword\">import</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> initializeApp <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"token string\">'firebase/app'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n<span class=\"token keyword\">import</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> getAI<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> getGenerativeModel<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> GoogleAIBackend<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> InferenceMode <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"token string\">'firebase/ai'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> app <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token function\">initializeApp</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>firebaseConfig<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n<span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> ai <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token function\">getAI</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>app<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">backend</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"token class-name\">GoogleAIBackend</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> model <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token function\">getGenerativeModel</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>ai<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">mode</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> InferenceMode<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token constant\">PREFER_ON_DEVICE</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"token comment\">// On a user interaction, before the first generateContent() call.</span>\ndownloadButton<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">addEventListener</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token string\">'click'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token operator\">=></span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n  model<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">initializeDeviceModel</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token parameter\">progress</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token operator\">=></span> <span class=\"token function\">renderProgress</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>progress<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n<span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> result <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">await</span> model<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">generateContent</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token string\">'Summarise this review in one line: ...'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\nconsole<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">log</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>result<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span>response<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span>inferenceSource<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span> <span class=\"token comment\">// 'ON_DEVICE' or 'IN_CLOUD'</span>\nconsole<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">log</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>result<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span>response<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">text</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>As you can see, the request is a normal <code class=\"language-text\">generateContent()</code> call. The mode decides where it runs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"language-text\">PREFER_ON_DEVICE</code> (the default): on-device when available, cloud otherwise.</li>\n<li><code class=\"language-text\">ONLY_ON_DEVICE</code>: on-device or it throws.</li>\n<li><code class=\"language-text\">PREFER_IN_CLOUD</code>: cloud when available, on-device otherwise (handy for offline).</li>\n<li><code class=\"language-text\">ONLY_IN_CLOUD</code>: cloud or it throws.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The on-device model is not there by default, and the first download can take several minutes. That's why <code class=\"language-text\">initializeDeviceModel()</code> exists: call it from a click that explains what's about to happen, not on page load.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>One API, two engines, and you decide the preference, not the plumbing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Tuning each side</h2>\n<p>The two engines take different knobs, so <code class=\"language-text\">getGenerativeModel()</code> accepts them separately:</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"js\"><pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\"><span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> model <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token function\">getGenerativeModel</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>ai<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">mode</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> InferenceMode<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token constant\">PREFER_ON_DEVICE</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">inCloudParams</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"token literal-property property\">model</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'gemini-3.5-flash-lite'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"token literal-property property\">generationConfig</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">maxOutputTokens</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token number\">200</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">onDeviceParams</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"token literal-property property\">createOptions</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"token literal-property property\">initialPrompts</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">[</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">role</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'system'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">content</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'You write short, neutral summaries.'</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">]</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n<span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><code class=\"language-text\">inCloudParams</code> follows the regular Gemini model params. <code class=\"language-text\">onDeviceParams.createOptions</code> maps to the Prompt API's <code class=\"language-text\">LanguageModel.create()</code> options, because under the hood the on-device side <strong>is</strong> Chrome's Prompt API. The model name above is the documented default for SDK v13; set it explicitly so an SDK upgrade doesn't change your model silently.</p>\n<h4>Structured output on both paths</h4>\n<p>This is my favourite part. One <code class=\"language-text\">Schema</code> object, used by both engines:</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"js\"><pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\"><span class=\"token keyword\">import</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> Schema <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">from</span> <span class=\"token string\">'firebase/ai'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> reviewSchema <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> Schema<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">object</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">properties</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"token literal-property property\">sentiment</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> Schema<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">enumString</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">enum</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">[</span><span class=\"token string\">'positive'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token string\">'neutral'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token string\">'negative'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">]</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"token literal-property property\">summary</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> Schema<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">string</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n<span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> model <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token function\">getGenerativeModel</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>ai<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">mode</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> InferenceMode<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token constant\">PREFER_ON_DEVICE</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">inCloudParams</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"token literal-property property\">generationConfig</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">responseMimeType</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'application/json'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">responseSchema</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> reviewSchema <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">onDeviceParams</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"token literal-property property\">promptOptions</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">responseConstraint</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> reviewSchema <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n<span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>The cloud side gets it as <code class=\"language-text\">responseSchema</code>, the device side as <code class=\"language-text\">responseConstraint</code>. The contract stays in one place, and you still validate the parsed result like any other untrusted input.</p>\n<h2>The limits of the on-device side</h2>\n<p>On-device supports single-turn text generation, text plus JPEG or PNG images, and structured output. It does <strong>not</strong> support:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multi-turn chat.</li>\n<li>Function calling, code execution, URL context, or grounding with Google Search or Maps.</li>\n<li>Audio, video or PDF input, or file URLs.</li>\n<li>Image generation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The maximum on-device token limit is 9216. With a <code class=\"language-text\">PREFER_*</code> mode, many of those requests fall back to the cloud; with <code class=\"language-text\">ONLY_ON_DEVICE</code>, they throw. A few things aren't supported in hybrid at all: the Live API, server prompt templates and token counting.</p>\n<p>One more gotcha: AI monitoring in the Firebase console doesn't show on-device requests. Log <code class=\"language-text\">inferenceSource</code> yourself, otherwise your dashboards will undercount.</p>\n<h2>App Check is the price of the cloud path</h2>\n<p>The cloud fallback calls the Gemini API straight from the browser, so you need App Check to prove the request comes from your app. On the web, the production provider is reCAPTCHA Enterprise. Firebase automatically enforced App Check for Firebase AI Logic on November 2, 2026, so unattested requests can get a <code class=\"language-text\">403</code>.</p>\n<p>For replay protection, SDK v12.14.0+ supports limited-use tokens:</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"js\"><pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\"><span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> ai <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token function\">getAI</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>app<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">backend</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">new</span> <span class=\"token class-name\">GoogleAIBackend</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n  <span class=\"token literal-property property\">useLimitedUseAppCheckTokens</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token boolean\">true</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n<span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Each token works once and the SDK fetches a new one per request, which can add latency. Start with \"Monitoring only\" in the console before enforcing replay protection. And keep App Check debug tokens out of the public repo and out of production builds. Trust me, it's easy to forget one in a <code class=\"language-text\">.env</code> file.</p>\n<p>To roll this out gradually, put it behind a flag. I cover that in <a href=\"/posts/feature-flagging-gemini-features-in-angular-with-ngx-feature-toggle/\">feature flagging Gemini features in Angular</a>, and the Angular streaming side is in <a href=\"/posts/streaming-gemini-responses-into-angular-signals/\">streaming Gemini responses into Angular signals</a>.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/ai-logic/hybrid-on-device-inference\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Build hybrid experiences with on-device and cloud-hosted models (Web)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/ai-logic/hybrid/web/configuration-options\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Configuration options for hybrid experiences in Web apps</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/reference/js/ai.hybridparams\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">HybridParams reference</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/ai-logic/app-check\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">App Check for Firebase AI Logic</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/prompt-api\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">The Prompt API in Chrome</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>That’s all for now</h3>\n<p>On-device first gives you privacy and zero per-call cost for the users who can run it. The cloud fallback makes sure everyone else still gets the feature.</p>\n<p>I hope you enjoyed this reading as much as I enjoyed writing it. Thank you so much for reading until the end and see you soon!</p>\n<p>🚀🚀🚀🚀🚀🚀</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Cya 👋</h3>","frontmatter":{"date":"June 11, 2026","path":"/posts/hybrid-inference-with-firebase-ai-logic-on-device-first-cloud-fallback/","title":"Hybrid inference with Firebase AI Logic — on-device first, cloud fallback","tags":["frontend","javascript","ai","gemini","chrome","architecture"],"status":"active"},"timeToRead":4}},"pageContext":{"postPath":"/posts/hybrid-inference-with-firebase-ai-logic-on-device-first-cloud-fallback/"}},"staticQueryHashes":["1271460761","63159454"],"slicesMap":{}}